<main id="main">

<!-- 01 · THREE DECISIONS (owner brief, 9 Nov 2026; shorter, 12 Nov): showroom, deliveries, in-season — one tab each -->
<section class="sec light" id="decision" aria-labelledby="decision-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span>01</span> One decision, made intelligently <em class="tagx">Illustrative</em></p>
    <h2 id="decision-h">What ORVUM puts in front of an owner.</h2>
    <p class="lead">Three moments decide a retail season: what you commit to in the showroom, which ready goods you pay for first, and how you protect margin on the stock you hold.</p>

    <div class="dtabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Three moments of a retail season">
      <button type="button" class="dtab" role="tab" id="dtab-a" aria-controls="dpanel-a" aria-selected="true"><span class="dtab-k">01A · Showroom</span><span class="dtab-n">OTB &amp; joint season plan</span></button>
      <button type="button" class="dtab" role="tab" id="dtab-b" aria-controls="dpanel-b" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"><span class="dtab-k">01B · Deliveries</span><span class="dtab-n">Ready goods &amp; cash velocity</span></button>
      <button type="button" class="dtab" role="tab" id="dtab-c" aria-controls="dpanel-c" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"><span class="dtab-k">01C · In-season</span><span class="dtab-n">Aged stock &amp; margin protection</span></button>
    </div>

    <div class="dpanel" id="dpanel-a" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="dtab-a" tabindex="0">
      <p class="dpanel-k">01A · Pre-season decision · Illustrative</p>
      <h3 class="dpanel-h">A key brand asks for 15% more budget (€480k → €552k), with 35% of the order in seasonal ready-to-wear (RTW).</h3>
    <article class="dcard" aria-label="Example ORVUM recommendation: open-to-buy and joint season plan">
      <div class="dcard-h"><span>ORVUM · OTB recommendation · ready for approval</span><span class="dcard-own">For the owner &amp; head buyer</span></div>
      <h4 class="dcard-t">Keep the overall budget. Rebalance this brand.</h4>
      <ul class="dsteps">
        <li><span>Brand open-to-buy</span> <strong>€480k → €420k (−12.5%)</strong></li>
        <li><span>Seasonal RTW</span> <strong>35% → 18% of the order</strong></li>
        <li><span>More weight into</span> <strong>Core footwear and knitwear, sizes 38–40 and M</strong></li>
        <li><span>Freed €60k goes to</span> <strong>A rising brand that sells 84% at full price</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="dk">Why — the evidence</p>
      <ul class="dev">
        <li><b>Buying</b> <span class="dfig">68% → 41%</span> full-price sell-through of its RTW in two seasons</li>
        <li><b>CRM</b> <span class="dfig">19 of 34 VIP clients</span> have moved their spending to two other houses</li>
        <li><b>Pricing</b> <span class="dfig">29% vs 62% target</span> realised gross margin on seasonal RTW</li>
        <li><b>Finance</b> <span class="dfig">€110k gap in October</span> if the larger order is accepted</li>
      </ul>
      <details class="dneg dmore">
        <summary>Alternatives, consequences &amp; the joint season plan</summary>
        <div class="dcard-g">
          <div>
            <p class="dk">More evidence</p>
            <ul class="dalt">
              <li><b>Category split</b>The plan’s category split has stayed the same while RTW sell-through fell.</li>
              <li><b>VIP clients</b>Of the 34 who bought this brand’s total looks two years ago, the other 15 still buy its footwear and knitwear at full price, but its seasonal dresses only on markdown.</li>
              <li><b>Cash</b>The 30% order deposit falls in the weeks of peak autumn supplier payments.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="dk">Alternatives ORVUM weighed</p>
            <ul class="dalt">
              <li><b>Repeat last season’s plan</b>Simple, but puts €117k more into slow RTW (€193k instead of €76k) — stock that has cleared only on markdown.</li>
              <li><b>Approve the full €552k</b>Adds €72k of buying capital, mostly in seasonal RTW, and leaves the €110k October cash gap open.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="dk">Expected consequences</p>
            <dl class="dcons">
              <div><dt>Buying capital (OTB)</dt><dd>€60,000 moved from slow-turning RTW to a rising brand that sells 84% at full price.</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Gross profit</dt><dd>Realised gross margin on this brand: +9% projected</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Cash</dt><dd>October supplier payments covered, without credit strain</dd></div>
            </dl>
            <p class="dk">Joint season plan for the brand meeting</p>
            <dl class="dcons">
              <div><dt>Position</dt><dd>€420k · RTW 18%</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Alternative</dt><dd>€450k · RTW 25%, if the brand prefers a broader mix. Both options keep the target margin.</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Shared goal</dt><dd>More of the collection sold at full price protects the brand’s image and the retailer’s margin. Its trousers in XS and L have gone to sale three seasons running, while footwear in 38–40 loses sales to missing stock. With the current split, about 45% of the collection would end the season on the discount rail.</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="dflow-note">In the designed workflow, after approval, the OTB ceiling is locked, the brand meeting brief is prepared, and committed cash is updated in Finance.</p>
      </details>
      <div class="dcard-f"><button type="button" class="dbtn" aria-disabled="true" aria-describedby="dnote-a">Approve OTB &amp; prepare the brand meeting brief</button><button type="button" class="dbtn o" aria-disabled="true" aria-describedby="dnote-a">Adjust split</button><button type="button" class="dbtn o" aria-disabled="true" aria-describedby="dnote-a">Reject</button><small id="dnote-a">Preview only — nothing can be approved here.</small><p class="dmsg" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p></div>
    </article>
    </div>

    <div class="dpanel" id="dpanel-b" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="dtab-b" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <p class="dpanel-k">01B · Delivery-window decision · Illustrative</p>
      <h3 class="dpanel-h">€310k of ready goods (at cost) wait for payment at three suppliers. This week’s release budget is €140k.</h3>
    <article class="dcard" aria-label="Example ORVUM recommendation: ready-goods release and cash">
      <div class="dcard-h"><span>ORVUM · Ready-goods release priority · ready for approval</span><span class="dcard-own">For the owner, buyer &amp; finance</span></div>
      <h4 class="dcard-t">Release the fast sellers today. Hold the winter outerwear.</h4>
      <ul class="dsteps">
        <li><span>Pay &amp; release today</span> <strong>Shipment B · €82k at cost · transitional footwear and denim</strong></li>
        <li><span>Pay &amp; release today</span> <strong>Part of Lot A1 · €58k at cost · core carry-over knitwear</strong></li>
        <li><span>Next release cycle</span> <strong>Rest of Lot A1 · €55k at cost · knitwear in sizes already in stock</strong></li>
        <li><span>Hold for 18 days</span> <strong>Shipment C · €115k at cost · heavy winter outerwear</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="dk">Why — the evidence</p>
      <ul class="dev">
        <li><b>Stores &amp; POS</b> <span class="dfig">≈ €14k of sales lost a week</span> missing sizes 38–41 in transitional footwear</li>
        <li><b>CRM</b> <span class="dfig">29 VIP clients waiting</span> for items in Shipment B and Lot A1</li>
        <li><b>Inventory</b> <span class="dfig">6.5 weeks of outerwear in stock</span> it sells only from mid-next month</li>
        <li><b>Finance</b> <span class="dfig">€105k back in 18 days</span> net cash from Shipment B + Lot A1</li>
      </ul>
      <details class="dneg dmore">
        <summary>Alternatives &amp; consequences</summary>
        <div class="dcard-g">
          <div>
            <p class="dk">More evidence</p>
            <ul class="dalt">
              <li><b>Clienteling</b>Clienteling appointments are already lined up for reserved pieces upon arrival, accelerating early full-price sell-through.</li>
              <li><b>Outerwear cash</b>Paying €115k today would hold that cash on the rails for 40+ days. Shipment C waits at the supplier’s warehouse; its supplier payment is due on Day 18.</li>
              <li><b>Net cash</b>Projected cash availability accounts for VAT, card-settlement timing, and ring-fenced store obligations. Priority releases are expected to fund most of Shipment C, with any remaining shortfall requiring confirmed available cash or agreed supplier terms before approval.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="dk">Alternatives ORVUM weighed</p>
            <ul class="dalt">
              <li><b>Pay by invoice date (Shipment C first)</b>Puts €115k into outerwear that sells later and delays fast-selling footwear by three weeks — so cash comes back later.</li>
              <li><b>Split €140k evenly across all three suppliers</b>Breaks size runs in every category, leaving stores with incomplete assortments and weaker sales.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="dk">Expected consequences</p>
            <dl class="dcons">
              <div><dt>Full-price sales</dt><dd>+€94k in the first three weeks, compared with paying by invoice date</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Cash velocity</dt><dd>€105,000 in net available cash (ex-VAT, cleared funds) projected within 18 days — covering 91% of the €115k supplier balance (at cost) for Shipment C. Shipment C is released only once cleared cash covers the full €115k, including the remaining €10k, before the supplier’s payment deadline — or under agreed revised supplier terms.</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Suppliers</dt><dd>Payment terms and shipping windows kept within agreed contract tolerances</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="dflow-note">In the designed workflow, after approval, Finance queues the supplier releases for Shipment B and Lot A1, Receiving prepares priority intake, and Shipment C is provisionally scheduled for the Day 18 cash cycle, subject to cleared cash for the full €115k before the supplier’s payment deadline or agreed revised terms.</p>
      </details>
      <div class="dcard-f"><button type="button" class="dbtn" aria-disabled="true" aria-describedby="dnote-b">Approve payment &amp; release queue</button><button type="button" class="dbtn o" aria-disabled="true" aria-describedby="dnote-b">Adjust tranches</button><button type="button" class="dbtn o" aria-disabled="true" aria-describedby="dnote-b">Reject</button><small id="dnote-b">Preview only — nothing can be approved here.</small><p class="dmsg" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p></div>
    </article>
    </div>

    <div class="dpanel" id="dpanel-c" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="dtab-c" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <p class="dpanel-k">01C · In-season decision · Illustrative</p>
      <h3 class="dpanel-h">Mid-season, sell-through is below plan: stock is ageing in one store, sizes are missing in another, and a supplier payment is coming.</h3>
    <article class="dcard" aria-label="Example ORVUM recommendation: aged stock and margin">
      <div class="dcard-h"><span>ORVUM · Recommendation · ready for approval</span><span class="dcard-own">For the owner · this week</span></div>
      <h4 class="dcard-t">Hold the price. Move the stock to where clients ask for it.</h4>
      <ul class="dsteps">
        <li><span>Autumn knitwear price</span> <strong>Hold for two more weeks</strong></li>
        <li><span>Move</span> <strong>18 pieces in sizes M–L · Store 3 → Store 7</strong></li>
        <li><span>Invite</span> <strong>11 clients who buy this brand</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="dk">Why — the evidence</p>
      <ul class="dev">
        <li><b>Sales &amp; stock</b> <span class="dfig">9% below plan</span> most of the unsold knitwear is in Store 3</li>
        <li><b>Stores &amp; POS</b> <span class="dfig">23 requests not served</span> for sizes M–L at Store 7 in three weeks</li>
        <li><b>CRM</b> <span class="dfig">11 clients ready to contact</span> bought this brand in M–L last season</li>
        <li><b>Finance</b> <span class="dfig">Payment due in 19 days</span> the plan still covers it</li>
      </ul>
      <details class="dneg dmore">
        <summary>Alternatives &amp; consequences</summary>
        <div class="dcard-g">
          <div>
            <p class="dk">Alternatives ORVUM weighed</p>
            <ul class="dalt">
              <li><b>Mark down 30% now</b>Clears stock faster, but gives up margin on knitwear that still has demand.</li>
              <li><b>Do nothing</b>Ageing stock grows, and a later markdown would have to be deeper.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="dk">Expected consequences</p>
            <dl class="dcons">
              <div><dt>Gross profit</dt><dd>More knitwear sold at full price — if clients respond</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Residual stock</dt><dd>Lower at season end</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Cash</dt><dd>Supplier payment still covered; a small transfer cost</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="dflow-note">In the designed workflow, after approval, store tasks are created, the decision is recorded, and the result is compared with this estimate in week 8.</p>
      </details>
      <div class="dcard-f"><button type="button" class="dbtn" aria-disabled="true" aria-describedby="dnote-c">Approve</button><button type="button" class="dbtn o" aria-disabled="true" aria-describedby="dnote-c">Adjust</button><button type="button" class="dbtn o" aria-disabled="true" aria-describedby="dnote-c">Reject</button><small id="dnote-c">Preview only — nothing can be approved here.</small><p class="dmsg" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p></div>
    </article>
    </div>

    <p class="dwhy"><strong>Why one system:</strong> each recommendation needs buying, stock, clients, commitments and cash read together — with the evidence shown and a person who approves.</p>

    <details class="dflow">
      <summary>How ORVUM reaches a decision — 4 steps</summary>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="fstep">
        <p class="fk"><span>1</span> Evidence</p>
        <h3>What ORVUM brings together</h3>
        <ul class="ev">
          <li><b>Inventory &amp; sales</b> by store, size and season — which stock is ageing, and where.</li>
          <li><b>Customer demand</b> — clients who buy this category, and the sizes they need that are missing.</li>
          <li><b>Receiving &amp; commitments</b> — what has arrived, what Delivery Match flagged, which purchase orders are still open.</li>
          <li><b>Cash obligations</b> — supplier payments due, set against expected sales.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="farrow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="fstep">
        <p class="fk"><span>2</span> Alternatives</p>
        <h3>Options, prepared for review</h3>
        <div class="opts" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Choose an option to see its consequences" id="opts">
          <button type="button" role="radio" aria-checked="true" data-o="clients">Targeted client engagement</button>
          <button type="button" role="radio" aria-checked="false" data-o="transfer" tabindex="-1">Transfers between stores</button>
          <button type="button" role="radio" aria-checked="false" data-o="markdown" tabindex="-1">Markdown timing</button>
          <button type="button" role="radio" aria-checked="false" data-o="buying" tabindex="-1">Buying adjustment</button>
        </div>
        <p class="ohint" id="o-what"></p>
      </div>
      <div class="farrow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="fstep">
        <p class="fk"><span>3</span> Consequences</p>
        <h3>Shown side by side, in their own units</h3>
        <dl class="cons" id="cons" aria-live="polite">
          <div><dt>Cash obligations</dt><dd data-c="cash"></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Expected gross profit</dt><dd data-c="gp"></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Residual stock</dt><dd data-c="stock"></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Working capital</dt><dd data-c="wc"></dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div class="farrow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="fstep fstep-end">
        <p class="fk"><span>4</span> Approval &amp; outcome</p>
        <h3>People decide. Results are checked.</h3>
        <ol class="appr">
          <li><b>Human approval</b> — the responsible person approves, adjusts or rejects. Nothing runs without approval, and only within that person's permissions.</li>
          <li><b>Execution record</b> — who approved what, when, and on which evidence.</li>
          <li><b>Measured outcome</b> — expected and realised results are compared at the agreed checkpoint.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    </details>

    <aside class="dsurface" aria-label="What this site shows"><p><strong>Only the visible surface.</strong> Three core decisions are shown here. The working prototype and deeper modules are shown privately, under NDA.</p></aside>
    <p class="note">Illustrative examples: stores, brands, figures and clients are invented. They show how ORVUM is designed to work; they do not describe a customer deployment or a measured result.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 · PROBLEM -->
<section class="sec dark" id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span>02</span> The problem</p>
    <h2 id="problem-h">Retail decisions are made in one place. Their consequences land in another.</h2>
    <p class="lead">Buying commits next season's cash months in advance. Stock ages in one store while the size a customer asks for is missing in another. Markdowns are set without seeing the supplier payments that follow. Every team sees its own part — almost no one sees the whole consequence in time to act.</p>
    <div class="cols3">
      <article class="pcard">
        <p class="pcard-k">Operations</p>
        <h3>Work runs in separate systems</h3>
        <p>Buying, receiving, stores, pricing and the team each use their own tools and spreadsheets. A delivery problem or a missing size reaches the shop floor before it reaches the people who can fix it.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="pcard">
        <p class="pcard-k">Information</p>
        <h3>Numbers arrive late and disagree</h3>
        <p>Sales, stock and customer data rarely match. Reports explain last month; they cannot show which store, size or client needs attention today — or which figures are incomplete.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="pcard">
        <p class="pcard-k">Financial consequences</p>
        <h3>Profit and cash are seen last</h3>
        <p>Gross profit, ageing stock, supplier commitments and cash needs become clear only after the season has moved on, when the remaining options are fewer and more expensive.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 · PLATFORM -->
<section class="sec light" id="platform" aria-labelledby="platform-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span>03</span> The platform</p>
    <h2 id="platform-h">One operating system for the whole retail chain.</h2>
    <p class="lead">ORVUM connects the work a retailer does every day — from the first buying decision to the cash it becomes — so every function works from the same product, stock and financial truth.</p>

    <div class="chain" role="group" aria-label="How ORVUM connects the retail chain">
      <div class="chain-layer chain-ai">
        <p class="chain-k">Approved AI recommendations</p>
        <p>Explain what is happening · recommend options with their consequences · act only after the responsible person approves</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="chain-flow" aria-label="The flow of goods and money">
        <li><span class="n">Buying</span><span class="d">Demand and buying capital</span></li>
        <li><span class="n">Purchase orders</span><span class="d">Commitments and terms</span></li>
        <li><span class="n">Receiving</span><span class="d">Delivery Match</span></li>
        <li><span class="n">Stock</span><span class="d">By store, size and age</span></li>
        <li><span class="n">Sales &amp; returns</span><span class="d">Stores and mobile POS</span></li>
        <li><span class="n">Customers</span><span class="d">Insight and clienteling</span></li>
      </ol>
      <div class="chain-layer chain-shared">
        <p class="chain-k">Shared across the chain</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Pricing</li><li>People</li><li>Planning</li><li>Finance</li><li>Analytics</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p class="chain-foot"><strong>One product identity.</strong> Every item carries one canonical record — style, colour, size, supplier, season and price — from the buying plan to the till, so each module counts the same thing.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="status"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>ORVUM is in development. Modules are being built and validated step by step; nothing on this site describes a live customer deployment.</p>
    <p class="contact-go"><a class="pbtn" href="/platform">How the platform is designed →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 · EXPLORE -->
<section class="sec dark" id="explore" aria-labelledby="explore-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span>04</span> Explore</p>
    <h2 id="explore-h">Intelligence across every retail function.</h2>
    <p class="lead">Select a function to see what it does, the decision it improves, what it connects to and the benefit it is designed to deliver.</p>

    <div class="ex" id="ex">
      <div class="ex-list" role="tablist" aria-orientation="vertical" aria-label="Retail functions" id="ex-list"></div>
      <div class="ex-panel" id="ex-panel" role="tabpanel" tabindex="-1" aria-live="polite"></div>
    </div>
    <noscript><p class="note">Turn on JavaScript to explore each function.</p></noscript>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 · RETAIL ECONOMICS -->
<section class="sec light" id="economics" aria-labelledby="economics-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span>05</span> Retail economics</p>
    <h2 id="economics-h">Designed around the measures that decide a retail year.</h2>
    <p class="lead">ORVUM is designed to help retailers improve five measures. Each is kept in its own unit — gross profit is not added to cash, and stock is not added to either.</p>
    <div class="econ">
      <article><h3>Gross profit</h3><p>Better-timed pricing and markdowns, so more of the season sells at a healthy margin.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Sell-through</h3><p>Stock matched to demand by store and size, and client opportunities surfaced while the product is still current.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Stock capital efficiency</h3><p>Slow and ageing stock identified early, so less capital stays locked in inventory that is not selling.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Working-capital planning</h3><p>Purchase-order commitments, deliveries and supplier payments seen together with expected sales — before cash gets tight.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Operational productivity</h3><p>Fewer manual reconciliations and hand-offs, with clear tasks and permissions for every role.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="note">These are design goals. ORVUM does not yet claim measured customer results.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 · LIVING INTELLIGENCE -->
<section class="sec dark" id="intelligence" aria-labelledby="intelligence-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span>06</span> Living intelligence</p>
    <h2 id="intelligence-h">An operating loop, not another report.</h2>
    <p class="lead">ORVUM is designed to work the way a strong retail leadership team works: evidence first, decisions owned by people, outcomes checked.</p>
    <ol class="loop">
      <li><span class="ln">01</span><h3>Evidence</h3><p>Facts from sales, stock, receiving, customers and finance, each traced to its source. Missing data is shown as missing.</p></li>
      <li><span class="ln">02</span><h3>Understanding</h3><p>What is changing, why, and what it means for profit, stock and cash.</p></li>
      <li><span class="ln">03</span><h3>Alternatives</h3><p>Realistic options, each with its consequences and trade-offs.</p></li>
      <li><span class="ln">04</span><h3>Authorised action</h3><p>The right person approves. ORVUM records and carries out only what was approved.</p></li>
      <li><span class="ln">05</span><h3>Measured outcome</h3><p>Expected and actual results are compared. The next decision starts from what was learned.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="loop-back" aria-hidden="true"><span>The next decision starts from the measured outcome</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 · FOUNDER -->
<section class="sec light" id="founder" aria-labelledby="founder-h">
  <div class="wrap founder">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow"><span>07</span> Founder</p>
      <h2 id="founder-h">Built from running retail, for retail.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="founder-t">
      <p>ORVUM was founded by Ilya Loshak, who has spent years operating luxury fashion retail — buying collections, running stores, and answering for stock, margin and cash.</p>
      <p>In that work, the decisions that mattered most were the ones no single system could see whole: what to buy, where stock should be, when to change a price, and what each choice would do to cash.</p>
      <p>ORVUM is designed for the wider retail market — independent retailers, retail groups and the brands they work with.</p>
      <p class="sig">Ilya Loshak <span>Founder, ORVUM</span></p>
      <p><a href="/about">Read about ORVUM →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 · TRUST -->
<section class="sec dark" id="trust" aria-labelledby="trust-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span>08</span> Trust</p>
    <h2 id="trust-h">Control is designed in, not added later.</h2>
    <div class="trust">
      <article><h3>Reliable data</h3><p>Every figure traces back to its source record. When data is missing or incomplete, ORVUM says so instead of filling the gap silently.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Role-based permissions</h3><p>Each person sees and does only what their role allows, by company and store. Each retailer's data is kept separate.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Human approval</h3><p>Recommendations never run on their own. People approve, adjust or reject them, and stay accountable for the decision.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Traceable outcomes</h3><p>Every decision is recorded with its evidence, approver and result, so it can be reviewed and audited later.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

